A||N||25||Monkeyman||Brakes--for the mechanically challenged||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-02-2000||09:31 AM||||Just got the GA brake upgrade from the Fiero Store and I have some questions already.<P>1)  Do I put both bearings <I>and</I> the other round ring thing in the hub before I install it on the car?<P>2)  How much grease do I need?  Do I just lube the bearings or do I need to actually fill the space inside?<P>3)  Do the outside of the slider tube things need to be greased before I insert them in the rubber parts?  Do the insides need to be greased, too?<P>4)  I noticed that there is a spring clip on the inside pad.  I know that this fits in the piston, but it doesn't fit tight.  Am I doing something wrong?  The outer pad doesn't fit snug either.  Won't these rub against the rotor?<P>I'm scared to death to do any brake work.  I figure, if I mess up something cosmetic, I'll just have a crappy looking car.  If I mess up the brakes, I won't be able to stop.<P><B>HELP!!!!</B><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||08-02-2000||10:41 AM||||  Hey Dave, not to worry. First off Yes the bearings need to de GREASED. You can either pack them by hand or, if you know someone that has a packing machine you can use, you can use that. Directions on packing by hand should be in your Chiltons or Haynes manual. Secondly, you dont need to grease the pin on the calliper, just  use and antiseize compound on the threads. The breaks get real hot and could cause the threads to bind. Lastly, that metal clip is the pad retainer and is mounted in a race on the piston, it should move freely. look at the piston, there should be four little notches on the outside edge where you can rotate the spring clip to remove it. To remove, just make sure that the four tines that are in the race line up with the notches or you could damage them (you don't have to remove them, that's just so you know incase you ever have to replace them). also, I believe that the spring clip has to sets of holding clips for the brake pad, one should be straight and the other should be contoured, both should protrude from the clip base at an angle. The sraight one go to the bottom and the contoured on top. Look at the piston, there should be two indentations in the piston head, those should line up with the nubs on the inner brake pad. What ever you do, dont listen to your brother-in-law when he tells you that they don't have to be lined up. My suspension upgrade just wound up costing me an extra $200.00 because my rear brakes were screwed when I went to reinstall them. Well good luck. I hope I accurately described thing for you. If not, then I'll refrain from any future advice or technical commentary. You always know that the Forum will answer you questions and if someone answers wrong, you know the flames will fly. Always get a second opinion befor starting anything. I learned that myself.<P> Late BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||12
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||08-02-2000||11:50 AM||||<BR>MonkeyMan, just in case you read the books and still are confused about packing the bearings here is what I do, hopefully this is clear, btw the instructions on based on me being right-handed.<P>First purchase some bearing grease, once you have the grease you might want some paper towels to wipe up any excess grease.<P>I place a wad of grease in the palm of my left hand, and take the bearing in my right hand.<P>Hold the bearing in your right hand with the smaller portion of the bearing at your fingers and the bigger portion of the bearing facing the grease.<P>You will push the bearing into the grease until the grease comes out the sides of the bearing. You will push and lift the bearing until the grease in coming out the side of the bearings.  Repeat the process until the whole bearing is packed, oosing with grease.<P>Simply applying grease to the exterior of the bearings is not good enough.  Bearings must be packed to allow proper mechanical operation and to prevent mechanical failure.<P>It is not difficult to pack bearings, but I can remember the first time that I worked on brakes, I was very timid.<P>As for the other stuff, I have not done the upgrade so I am at a loss.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 08-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-02-2000||12:00 PM||||Sounds messy.  This is a job for the garage, not the living room.  I just cleaned out my change and I think I have enough to buy the rear brakes and paint tomorrow.  MC next week, then I can put it all together.  Thanx for the help, guys.  Keep the answers coming.||||reg||1
Z||000004||b lo 0||08-02-2000||12:08 PM||||Yeah, before I sold my old Nissan, I had to replace the rotors and brake pads.  Gold-86SE is right on.  Once you get into it, it's actually kind of fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Sometimes, it's faster though to kinda "slap" the bearing on your hand. But be sure to move it around and always have plenty of grease in your palm.  Well, have fun packing!<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">    <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-02-2000||12:47 PM||||Another coupla questions...<P>1)  The glide pins appear to be slightly longer than the rubber ?bushing?.  Where should the excess pin go?  On the outside or inside or does it matter?<P>2)  How much of the caliper can I paint?<P>3)  What keeps the outside pad from constantly rubbing the rotor?  It just sits there loosely.||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-02-2000||01:25 PM||||Well, the outside pads are retained by the outside of the caliper. Yes, they do drag the rotor very slightly, that's why it's important to use "brake stop-squeek." It acts as a glue to keep the pads off the rotor as much as possible.<P>The proximity relationship between the guide pins and the caliper is predicated by pad wear. In english, that would read that as the outside pad wears, the caliper slides on the pins.<P>paint as much as you want, with exception of guide pins. I'm not a big paint freak myself, although it does look good if you have wheels that expose the rotors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||08-02-2000||02:02 PM||||Good luck on your brake job. It's easy, don't sweat it.<P>The only additions to the above I can think of are:<BR>Use Molly grease as it has anti-wear properties on the bearings. Wear rubber gloves.<BR>Make sure you only use disk-brake grease (it's also a special product) on the calipers. See the manuals for where to apply.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-02-2000||03:21 PM||||Don--I don't see any races on the inside of the piston.  It's all smooth.  Also, I only got one extra clip.  It was included in the glide pin package.  Am I missing one?  Or do I have something that I don't really need?  The inner pad is still loose on both calipers.  I'm planning on getting the parts for the rear tomorrow so maybe the other parts will fit better and I'll have an idea what's going on with these.<P>EdsB52--I just found the little tube of anti-squeek.  I'll have to remember to use that.  I have to paint the calipers.  I need the extra 15 hp.  Seriously, the GA calipers are supposed to be steel so they rust?  I have aftermarket rims so the calipers will show through.  I want them to look as good as possible.||||reg||1
Z||000009||BAM-BAM||08-02-2000||04:03 PM||||  Dave, if that's the case then I would contact the Fiero Store by phone and ask someone where the other spring clip is. Also ask them how it is supposed to mount to the calliper. It's not a good thing to have the pads in loose they tend to bounce around and bind. The pads should both mount solid in the calliper. Sorry for any misinformation. Also, the bearing seal goes on the inside of the hub. the smaller bearing goes on the outside with the retaining washer and spindle nut. Make sure you install the inner bearing before you install the seal and be  carefull installing the seal, you dont want it to go in crooked or it will distort. Then you'll have to get new seals.<P>  Later, BAM<P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||12
Z||000010||batboy||08-02-2000||07:48 PM||||I'm sorry, but I have to seriously disagree with Bam-Bam's comment that the slider pins/bolts don't need grease. They absolutely do need a touch of high temperature synthetic grease. Pull the slider down one way, smear some grease on the inside, slide it the other way, force some grease under the sliding part by the threads the best you can, etc. Work it back and forth a few times to get it worked in good.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 08-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||EdsB52||08-02-2000||10:42 PM||||Monkeyman, to expound on my statement for use of anti-squeek, I would say that that stuuf usually sets up fairly quick and could impede the correct seating of the pads inside the caliper if it were allowed to congeal before you wanted it to. So make sure and install pads with the goop, install calipers, and then give the brake pedal a good shove to seat the pads to the rotor and caliper. I would let it set for fifteen minutes or so, so the goop dries before operation. I'm probably making this sound far more critical than it really is, but that's the difference between doing a good job. and doing a perfect job.<P>About the lubricating of the sliders. I would definately lube 'em. As Batboy said, just a small amount. If you think a little is good, but more is better, you'll gunk the sliders and wear out the inside pad long before the outside. Have fun. <P>P.S. Good one about extra 15 HP....LOL||||reg||1
Z||000012||BAM-BAM||08-02-2000||11:41 PM||||   Ok alow me to defend myself. I, personaly, have never used any lube on the slider portion of the calliper attachment bolts. With the lack of moisture here in southern Nevada there isn't alot of opportunity for corrosive binding. However, I do agree with BatBoy and ED that it wouldn't hurt anything especially in damper climates like those found in the MidWest and the Valley of the Sun, Pheonix. I, Personaly, have had no incodence of bind in this particular area. Again, Monk, sorry for any misinformation. Do the others agree with the antiseize compound on the threads? I may be wrong here also. So, hence forth, I shall refrain from providing misguided and obliviously bad advice. please accept my most humble appology.<P> bam<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 08-02-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000013||EdsB52||08-03-2000||12:19 AM||||Off topic;<P>Bam-Bam. You stated "damper climates" and included the valley of the sun, Phoenix. Come on buddy, we have identical climates (Southern Nevada and Phoenix), unless you were kidding. See ya, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||08-03-2000||02:03 AM||||Assuming I can still remove the sliders from their rubber mounts/gaskets/whatever-they-are, I'll be sure to put a touch of grease on them.  I guess it couldn't hurt.<P>I'm still a little unsure of the correct mounting for the pads.  The inside pad has a clip attached to it.  It just doesn't fit tightly in the piston.  The outer pad doesn't have any clip attached to it.  Does the anti-squeek/glue go on the inner or outer pad?  I changed the brake pads on my wifes car (OK...I helped a friend.  OK...I got his tools for him.).  It seems that there were some tabs on the outer pad that attached it to the caliper.  This seems to be the questionable part in my mind (what's left of it).  Would it help if I uploaded some pics of what I've got?  I could do that tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||08-03-2000||07:34 AM||||I've only ever used that brake pad adhesive on the inner pads. The outer pads should have their own built in clips that snap fairly securely to the caliper housing.<P>I'm puzzled by your questions about the slider pins being longer than a rubber bushing??? Did the kit give you 2 new sliders per caliper, or are you supposed to use your old ones. Just so we are on the same page, the slider pins are the same thing as the bolts that actually hold the caliper onto the car. These just push out of the old caliper. If they seem a little stuck, carefully tap them out with a punch and small hammer.<P>The new calipers should have two new little rubber O-rings per slider hole. If these are not already installed, they have to be carefully placed into the little grooves inside the bolt holes. Is this making any sense?<P>About the hubs, you need to install both the inner and outer bearing races, if this is not already done. They have to be driven in evenly and be very careful not to damage them. Once both races are in each of the two hubs, pack the wheel bearings with a high temperature wheel bearing grease designed for use with disc brakes. Install the inner bearings and carefully drive in the dust seal on the inner sides. If you damage the seal, which is easy to do, you need to buy new ones. The outer bearings go on after you slip the hub onto the spindle. A big washer and a nut with a cotter pin go onto the end and you need to do a special torquing process that is described in the manual. I sure hope this helps, you're starting to scare me.||||reg||1
Z||000016||BAM-BAM||08-03-2000||07:38 AM||||  Ed, Phoenix is a damper climate than southern Nevada in that starting in July, you all have the summer monsoons. Here in Vegas, if we get a monsoon it's kind of like spitting of the Queen Mary. Not trying to rattle any cages, but I was stationed in Chandler (WAFB) from 81 to 86 and then transfered to Nellis. My entire time here I have not seen quite the moisture we had in the Phoenix valley. Phoenix is A much  "GREENER" desert. I guess it all boils down to a matter of preference.<P>  Later, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> P.S. We have been over 150 days without any measurable rain.||||reg||12
Z||000017||Monkeyman||08-03-2000||10:37 AM||||BB--As you can see, the outer pads don't have any clips at all on them.  Just the parts sticking out to align them.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brakepads.JPG"> <P>About the slider pins.  I'm referring to the metal tubes the bolts go into.  As you can see from the 1st pic, they are about 1/8" shy of being flush with the rubber "bushing".  (BTW--It looks as though the rubber is one piece that goes clear through.)  In the 2nd pic, you can see where they seem to be alot more than 1/8" from being flush.  (This was as far as I could get them to go with my bare hands.)  Will this be a problem?  Do I need to remove this part and grease the outside of the "tube"?  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newcaliper.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newcaliper2.JPG"> <P>A question about the "races".  I'm still unsure exactly what these are.  Maybe they are already installed?<P>Can you (or anyone) describe the "special torquing process" so that I know what to look for?<P>I appreciate everyones help.  I've been scared about this for a while.  If I can't figure this out, I guess I'll have a conversion kit to sell.  There isn't anyone around here willing to do anything other than stock work.<P>PS--I just took a closer look at the hub.  Are the races the smooth, round metal things the bearings rest on?  If so, they're already pressed into the hub.||||reg||1
Z||000018||webbee||08-03-2000||06:00 PM||||Yes, the races are the smooth metal rings that the bearings ride on. You drive them out, from behind, with a drift punch. You can use a socket to drive the new one in with. New bearings, new race, it must be done. The new races are included with your new bearings. It's easy, 15 mins per race. <P>The torquing process is in the manuals.||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||08-03-2000||07:59 PM||||Ok, I assumed those were Grand Am calipers, but apparently they're not. I've never seen anything quite like those. Better find out what they're really made for, then go to the library and copy a few pages out of a manual or something. I've never seen GM sliders or bushings like that either. Regardless, it's the same principal. Pretend it's a puzzle and piece it together before trying to install them. Look at the back of the outer brake pad. See those bumps? Those match up with the recessed areas on the caliper. Looks like maybe with that style, you may have to use the brake pad goop on both pads after all. Sounds like your bearing races might be already installed. If they look new, there is no reason to take them out as Webbee says. He probably don't understand these are new hubs. <P>Bam-Bam, I didn't mean to sound like I was knocking you, trust me, I've given the wrong advice before too and have been corrected in the past...we're all human. But, regardless of what climate you live in, those floater pins slide metal against metal. Think about it. Metal constantly rubbing back and forth against metal. Yes, you do need grease.||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||08-03-2000||08:36 PM||||I didn't see this answer above. Hopefully it's not to late.<P>Do Not pack the area between the bearings full of grease. A little grease in there is fine but don't fill it. If you do you'll leak grease either from the seal or the metal cap as the hub heats up.<P>Some people will tell you that grease stuffed in that area will add to the grease in the bearings. In general it's waste. Wheel bearing grease is designed not to migrate when it gets hot.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Eric_PA||08-03-2000||10:26 PM||||I got the same brake upgrade and called up the fiero store. They told me that they are from a 91 Berretta gt. They rust fast, mine did. I'm going to paint them soon. Another problem that I have with them is that the brake pedal now travels much farther and seems to set the rear brakes before the front. I bought the proportioning valve right away, just didn't put it in yet. As far as the clip goes, there was one on each of the inner pads. I didn't like the way they felt, a little lose. The outer pads don't have any clips, the tabs are extra long and stick all the way through the caliper. I never seen any brakes like it before. Overall the car stops better, but still get brake fade.<P>Eric<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||webbee||08-03-2000||10:50 PM||||If the races are new you don't need to replace them. I didn't know that the hubs were new. ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Monkeyman||08-04-2000||02:11 AM||||Everything's new.  (Well...the hubs are cut down, reconditioned Fiero units.) <P>BB--I assumed the same thing until I gave Justin Cote (at the Fiero Store) a call today.  '91 Beretta parts.  Justin said that he only uses the goop on the outer pad.  He said that no one has ever complained about either the pads or the rotors wearing funny or quickly, but the pads do ride on the rotors.  Hmmm...  Not really what I wanted to hear, but if that's the way the Berettas were set up, it must be OK.  <P>I understand that I need to grease the sliders.  Do I apply the grease to the inside of the tube or between the tube and the caliper?<P>Ogre--You're definitely not too late.  I'm still trying to get around to getting the rear parts.  Hopefully in the next few days.  Then I'll paint the calipers before I install them.  Of course, it'll take another few days before I can get up the courage to actually do this.<P>Eric_PA--I knew the calipers would rust so I plan to paint mine before I install them.  I just wish I could find some light purple hi-temp paint to contrast with the darker purple on the car.  I think you can solve the pedal travel problem with a new MC.  Randy Agee (brake conversion guru) used one from a full size Blazer.  I'll be getting one with the rear brakes.  <P>You guys are great.  Maybe I'll actually accomplish this correctly after all. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||DJRice||08-04-2000||09:07 AM||||Monkey, <BR>When the time comes, you will do ok.  Just pay attention to how you get your original brakes off as all disc brakes are pretty close to the same, with only a few minor differences as evidenced by this thread.  <P>I know after I did my first brake job I was irritated that I actually paid big bucks for someone else to do them in the past.  <P>If you have some free time, maybe some "exploratory surgery" on the monkeymobile is in order.  It could help you aviod potential problem areas and if you do run across a problem you can ask more questions here before you dismantle the grape ape.  <P>And by the way, expect to lose skin from a couple of your knuckles, thats just the way it goes!<P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"Matched Perfect and Staggered Special"||||reg||1
Z||000025||Monkeyman||08-04-2000||01:29 PM||||I have very little free time these days, but I never thought of "experimenting" on the Monkeymobile.  Good idea.  If I get a chance, that's just what I'll do.  ||||reg||1
